# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_INTERRUPT_CREATE */
int64_t interrupt_create(uint32_t irq, uint64_t number, uint32_t options,
                         uint32_t *out);
/* CROI_SYS_INTERRUPT_ACK */
int64_t interrupt_ack(croi_interrupt_ack_item_t *items, uint64_t count);
/* CROI_SYS_INTERRUPT_SET_AFFINITY */
int64_t interrupt_set_affinity(uint32_t interrupt, uint64_t cpu_mask);

int64_t croi_interrupt_ack(uint32_t interrupt);
```

# DESCRIPTION

An interrupt object stands for one device interrupt.
A driver binds it to a port with object_bind(2) (`CROI_BIND_INTERRUPT`, to a port made with `CROI_PORT_BIND_TO_INTERRUPT`), and each time the interrupt fires, one `CROI_PKT_TYPE_INTERRUPT` packet arrives with the time in nanoseconds in payload[0].
The interrupt then waits to be acknowledged: a further firing meanwhile only records its time, and is delivered at the acknowledgment.
A level-triggered interrupt is masked at the controller when it fires and unmasked on the acknowledgment, so it cannot storm while the driver works.
An interrupt that fires before it is bound is delivered at the binding.
Closing the last handle destroys the interrupt.

## interrupt_create

`interrupt_create` writes to <out> a handle to an interrupt object for <number>.
<irq> is a resource(2) of kind IRQ that covers <number>, or the root resource.
<number> is the interrupt's GSI on amd64 (an I/O APIC pin), its INTID on arm64 (a GICv3 SPI, 32 and up) and its source on the PLIC on rv64.
<options> is any of:

: `CROI_INTERRUPT_REMAP_IRQ`
  <number> is an ISA IRQ, which the MADT's overrides map to a GSI (amd64).
: `CROI_INTERRUPT_MODE_DEFAULT`, `_EDGE_LOW`, `_EDGE_HIGH`, `_LEVEL_LOW`, `_LEVEL_HIGH`
  The trigger mode.
  The default is what firmware says (on amd64 the MADT overrides, else ISA edge-high and PCI level-low).
  `CROI_INTERRUPT_MODE_EDGE_BOTH` is `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.
: `CROI_INTERRUPT_TIMESTAMP_MONO`
  Accepted: the times are monotonic anyway.

Another option, or a number the controller does not have, is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
The interrupt is routed to the boot CPU and unmasked.
The handle has the basic rights, READ, WRITE and SIGNAL.

## interrupt_ack

`interrupt_ack` acknowledges each of up to 64 bound interrupts, each item a `croi_interrupt_ack_item_t`:

```c
typedef struct {
  uint32_t interrupt;  // the interrupt's handle
  int32_t status;      // out: 0, or why this item failed
} croi_interrupt_ack_item_t;
```

Each handle needs WRITE.
An interrupt not bound to a port is `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`; one destroyed is `CROI_ERR_CANCELED`.
Items are all done.
The call returns how many succeeded and writes each item's <status>.
`croi_interrupt_ack` acknowledges one and returns 0, the item's status or the call's.

## interrupt_set_affinity

`interrupt_set_affinity` routes the interrupt to the lowest-numbered CPU of <cpu_mask> (bit n is CPU n) that takes interrupts and is not reserved to another job (job(2)).
Until it is called the interrupt goes to the boot CPU.
The handle needs WRITE.
No usable CPU in the mask is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`; an interrupt that cannot be moved to a CPU is `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.

